ll take a look at how the las  vegas raiders are  helping out in the community  one of the bright spots in this pandemic  time is the las vegas raiders  the team is on the field in their new  stadium of course but the guys are also  very busy off the field  kevin martin joins us now to talk about  how the raiders have helped make times a  little easier for so many  in our community the raiders are  officially at the midway point of the  season  i think it goes without saying that so  far this has been one of the more  unconventional nfl seasons ever played  but the raiders have made the most of it  not just on the field  but also off of it making it a point to  still have a positive impact on the las  vegas community  here's a look back at the ways the  raiders were involved through the first  eight weeks of the season  tight end jason whitten took part in a  panel that discussed ways for the las  vegas community  to move forward united during the black  lives matter  protests everything that you would want  to be a part of  before i showed up if you said how would  you want it to go  not knowing what was going to happen i  would say exactly that i heard passion i  heard frustration i saw accountability  kicker daniel carlson and defensive back  lamarcus joyner spoke virtually to a  group of student athletes from liberty  high school  to be able to have our faith in our  voice and community  and giving back you know paying it  forward how it was given to us  i mean that's spiritual healing to me  and a group of raider alumni made a stop  at the metro police department k-9  division  to meet k-9 raider the newest addition  to the unit  who was purchased by the raiders and  several raiders including nelson aguiler  took part in a virtual escape room with  a special group of teens from the candle  lighters childhood cancer foundation and  the las vegas raiders are also  donating a five thousand dollars well  not donating they're giving a five  thousand dollar grant to the candlelight  program so thank you guys so much this  is just eight weeks into the inaugural  season and i think we all can agree  that we can't wait to see the silver and  black continue to have a positive impact  on our community  covering the raiders in las vegas i'm  kevin martin  and still ahead staying busy is a great  way to help bring normalcy to these  very unusual times we're going to  introduce you to a georgia woman taking  her skills to the outdoors  christmas  and a message from santa in finland on  what his hope for christmas celebrations  will be  well as we know once the pandemic hit  thousands of restaurants were just  forced to shut down  and if you're a food writer and there's  no food to write about well things can  look pretty bleak  unless you think off the menu here's  steve hartman  for atlanta-based food writer angela  hansberger  this pandemic has been hard to swallow  can't write about restaurants when  they're closed  i was facing a lot of anxiety i kind of  hit it  um gosh i'm crying  i was really sad for all the people in  the industry that i worked with  angela says she had to get her mind off  it tarragon  so using ingredients from her garden and  her encyclopedic knowledge of fine  dining  angela opened her own restaurant on her  front  porch where she now serves chipmunk  this chipmunk the little guy is at a  standing lunch reservation for months  now  whether it's veggie pizza and a side  salad or imitation  sushi with walnut wontons every day  angela goes to remarkable lengths for  her exclusive clientele  took a while to try and make little  tortillas  that i could fold to put the to put the  egg  i love the way that you're shaking well  i love how you're realizing how crazy it  sounds as you're saying it  today it's mini spaghetti with tiny date  meatballs  and a thimbleful of breadsticks the  chipmunk who she named  thelonious monk typically arrives within  seconds of serving  eats his fill and packs the rest to go  in his cheeky doggy bag  and the reviews have been good the  reviews have been great  one day he came up with this little tiny  bundle wadded up  leaves and put it on the table so i take  it to me and he brought  a tip still you get the sense that he  remains  skeptical like there's got to be a catch  angela's cat wishes there was  a catch but there will be none of that  because for angela this little bit of  silliness  has become her daily sustenance what has  it done for you  it's brought joy i have more videos of  this chipmunk than i have of my children  we all need to find a way to cope during  this pandemic  which is why i'm giving shay angela my  highest  rodent restaurant rating three stripes  steve hartman cbs news on the road  that is a world-class restaurant to that  chipmunk that is hilarious  finally tonight a very special message  from santa claus himself  the pandemic may change the way so many  celebrate the holidays but  we checked in with the big man and he  said there's no way the holidays are  cancelled  but we have to follow guidelines one of  the  most wonderful aspects of christmas  is spending time together that's true  certainly we have to follow and respect  all the public health recommendations  santa  what is your wish for this christmas  i hope we all stay safe  and i wish to see you soon be good  that's a message coming from finland's  arctic wonderland which usually attracts  many families each and every year but  santa's post office there receives  thousands of letters from kids across  the globe revealing  their wishes for christmas thank you so  much for watching our community pride  special stronger together  yeah we hope to serve as a reminder of  the resilience nevadans have thanks for  watching  you
 

